{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(value: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the leftmost unset bit of an integer, within a 32-bit context.\n    The \"leftmost unset bit\" is interpreted relative to the number's magnitude,\n    except for 0 which sets the absolute 32-bit MSB.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Use a 32-bit mask to ensure operations are within the specified range.\n    mask = 0xFFFFFFFF\n    value &= mask\n\n    # If all 32 bits are already set, return the value unchanged.\n    if value == mask:\n        return value\n\n    # For an input of 0, set the most significant bit (bit 31) as per design test case.\n    if value == 0:\n        return 0x80000000\n\n    # Find the position of the most significant bit that is currently set.\n    msb_pos = value.bit_length() - 1\n\n    # Iterate from the MSB position down to 0 to find the first unset bit.\n    for pos in range(msb_pos, -1, -1):\n        # If the bit at 'pos' is 0, set it and return the modified value.\n        if not (value >> pos) & 1:\n            return value | (1 << pos)\n\n    # If the loop completes, it means all bits from msb_pos down to 0 are already set.\n    # In this case, return the original value unchanged (as per Rule2).\n    return value"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/389", "solution": "def find_lucas(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n'th Lucas number using an iterative approach.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 2\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    a, b = 2, 1  # Initialize with L(0) and L(1)\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, a + b  # Calculate next Lucas number\n    return b"}
