{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "import math\ndef max_sum(arr: list) -> int:\n    n = len(arr)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n < 3:\n        return sum(arr)\n\n    # lis_sum[i] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[i].\n    lis_sum = [arr[i] for i in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lis_sum[i] = max(lis_sum[i], arr[i] + lis_sum[j])\n\n    # lds_sum[i] stores the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at arr[i].\n    lds_sum = [arr[i] for i in range(n)]\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lds_sum[i] = max(lds_sum[i], arr[i] + lds_sum[j])\n\n    max_result = -math.inf # Initialize with negative infinity to handle any possible integer sums.\n\n    # A bitonic subsequence can be purely increasing, purely decreasing,\n    # or increasing-then-decreasing with a peak. We find the maximum among all these.\n    for i in range(n):\n        # Case 1: Purely increasing subsequence ending at index i.\n        max_result = max(max_result, lis_sum[i])\n        \n        # Case 2: Purely decreasing subsequence starting at index i.\n        max_result = max(max_result, lds_sum[i])\n        \n        # Case 3: Increasing-then-decreasing subsequence with arr[i] as the peak.\n        # This requires arr[i] to have at least one element smaller than it to its left\n        # (indicated by lis_sum[i] > arr[i]) and at least one element smaller than it\n        # to its right (indicated by lds_sum[i] > arr[i]).\n        if lis_sum[i] > arr[i] and lds_sum[i] > arr[i]:\n            max_result = max(max_result, lis_sum[i] + lds_sum[i] - arr[i])\n            \n    return max_result"}
