{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "import math\ndef max_sum_increasing_subseq(arr, n, i, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at or before index i,\n    and also including the element at index k (where k > i).\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr or n == 0 or k >= n or i >= n or k <= i:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[j] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at index j\n    dp = [0] * n\n\n    # Initialize dp[j] with arr[j] itself, as a single element is an increasing subsequence\n    for x in range(n):\n        dp[x] = arr[x]\n\n    # Fill dp array for elements up to index i (inclusive)\n    for current_idx in range(i + 1):\n        for prev_idx in range(current_idx):\n            if arr[current_idx] > arr[prev_idx]:\n                dp[current_idx] = max(dp[current_idx], dp[prev_idx] + arr[current_idx])\n\n    # Now, we need to consider subsequences that include arr[k].\n    # The subsequence must start before or at 'i' and end at 'k', being increasing.\n    # The max_sum should be initialized with arr[k] in case arr[k] itself is the longest (or only) subsequence.\n    max_sum_with_k = arr[k]\n\n    # Iterate through elements from prefix (up to i)\n    for prefix_idx in range(i + 1):\n        # If arr[k] can extend an increasing subsequence ending at prefix_idx\n        if arr[k] > arr[prefix_idx]:\n            max_sum_with_k = max(max_sum_with_k, dp[prefix_idx] + arr[k])\n    \n    # If arr[k] is the smallest element or cannot extend any subsequence from prefix,\n    # it can still form a subsequence by itself (with sum arr[k]).\n    return max_sum_with_k"}
